 dr hopkins is we wouldn't know about any  of this  without the huge testing capacity and  the sequencing capacity  you know we sequence uh just under half  of all these  genetic sequences in the world in this  country  because of that capacity that we've  built um and in fact i was in glasgow  yesterday i was seeing the  expansion of that sequencing which  there which we're putting in place to to  increase it further  it's only because of that capacity that  we were able to find these six  in most other countries in the world we  simply wouldn't have known that that had  happened  and therefore we were able to track all  six down  dr hopkins thank you so i think the uh  challenge about borders is that people  coming into this country at the moment  and leaving should only be doing it for  essential reasons and as you know  only people who have a british residency  or a british passport can come in  from many countries right now in  addition the day two  and the day eight tests while people are  in quarantine give us  extra reassurance of being able to  detect cases  and we are also whole genome sequencing  any of those that can be sequenced  all of that is really positive because  it allows us to find any of these  variants coming into the country  earlier trace any of their contacts  trace people on flights as we did for  the previous cases  and that gives us a lot of reassurance  that even with small numbers of  individuals coming into the country  that we can minimize the risk to the  public health and the population  uh thanks very much next question nigel  morris from the eye  nigel um secretary of state um  the pm said all the way back in july  2019 that the government had already  made plans for  fixing the long-running crisis and  social care funding  and yet his press secretary said  yesterday that you still had no  detailed proposals on the issue could  you finally  give us a firm date when your social  care reform plans will be produced  and update us on what uh cross-party  contacts have taken place  um following your call for cross-party  consensus on the issue  and secondly very quickly could i ask  was consideration given to  a one-off bonus to nhs staff as  is happening in scotland and if so why  was the idea rejected  well thanks very much the um the  obviously  solving the social care funding issue  has been a long-standing problem we have  a clear manifesto  commitment to deliver on that and we  intend to publish  details of how we plan to do that  this year i've been working with the  prime minister  on it clearly before the pandemic we  were  starting to work immediately after the  election on this  we we did engage then in cross-party  engagement i put out a call as you  mentioned for cross-party  suggestions and input we've received  uh quite a lot of that and that's been  part of our  considerations and i hope that we can  come forward  with a plan that can work and can carry  um public broad consensus now  it is more challenging to deliver this  in more difficult  economic times that is obvious and  clear what we need to do though is put  in place  a long-term plan that will bring about a  solution to this problem at the same  time  there are many many other improvements  to the delivery of social care that we  can make  as well as addressing this issue of the  funding reform  and many of those are contained in the  white paper that i set out last month  on which there will be a bill in due  course and to bring  an integration between health and social  care formally and much more so than  we've had  uh until now because that is part  of the solution to this uh significant  problem um and uh even that's part of  the solution the integration of health  and social care  is an issue that's eluded governments of  all stripes  over many many years and i'm absolutely  delighted to have been able to set out  that white paper  and uh to bring forward the bill uh in  due course  um on the um uh on the second party i've  completely forgotten your second  question i should have written it down  sorry  one off bonus for scots oh yes um the  the the of course um the  administration in edinburgh uh take  their decisions in terms of  uh in terms of how they run the nhs  and that isn't the approach that we  have chosen to take here uh and  obviously the matters of pay will will  go  to the uh pay review body um  but we have set out what is affordable  given the very significant challenges to  the public finances  next question is john johnson from  politics home  uh good afternoon health secretary uh  two quick questions if i may  um earlier today the scottish  conservative leader  douglas ross said the test and trace in  the early stages of the pandemic  did not work as well as it should have  and that he believed  ministers would accept that do you  accept that analysis  and if not why not and secondly  conservative mps including the chair of  the education select committee  have called for further evidence to be  published over the weekend  regarding the use of face masks in  schools  do you have any plans to explain to  parents and teachers why  that guidance has been issued to  reassure them that when pupils go back  on monday that they're making the right  decision by asking  children to wear face masks thanks john  on that second question i'm going to ask  susan to give a clinical answer on the  first question  of course at the start of this pandemic  test and trace didn't exist  we had we developed the first test  public health england did that and they  did it within a  about a month of first discovering about  this virus  in the first place and so of course  there's been a  program of constant improvement and it's  testament to that improvement that we  now have  turnaround times of under 24 hours in  the vast majority of tests  right across the uk we have this huge  sequencing capacity which is  the biggest in the world as a proportion  of our population  by a huge margin we have  contact tracing that reaches over  90 percent of positive cases and over 90  percent of their  of their contacts and again very very  rapid speeds  which is a an incredible achievement of  the team to have built all of this  it means that we are in a better place  to deal with a pandemic like this  than before and it's been because we've  built it  all the way through that's been a very  important part of what we've  uh of what we've had to do and i've  spoken on this podium many times about  the work  that's gone on to make all of that  happen  susan thank you so our face coverings  and face masks  we have reviewed the evidence in the and  also looked at  the variant that we have circulating  b117  it was with that variant and with the  increased transmissions  that we were seeing around the school  environment not just in the school but  on the way to school especially in  secondary schools  that the advice to for secondary school  children to wear masks in the classroom  and in corridors whilst moving around  the school came as part of the advice  package to reduce the risk  in secondary schools on the return on  march the 8th  that goes hand in hand with the testing  that we're advising schools to do to  also reduce the risk  along with all of the other measures the  schools have put into play  and this is all there to reduce the risk  of transmission in schools to allow our  children to  continue their education safely and to  ensure that we have  the least amount of rises that we can  possibly hope for  with increased mixing around return to  schools next week  thanks very much susan final question  megan ford from the nursing times  good evening nurses are considering  industrial action  and others may quit altogether following  the news that they might receive just a  one percent pay rise  after a year of covered 19. so my  question is  how do you expect to stabilize the nhs  and complete the vaccination program  without nurses on the ground well megan  it's an incredibly important point that  we need  more nurses not just the nurses that we  have now and we have a  clear manifesto commitment to 50 000  more nurses in the nhs  and i'm delighted that there's more than  10 000 more nurses  who've joined the nhs over the last 12  months  also we've seen an increase in 34  percent of more than a third in the  applications  to nursing and of course over the past  three years nurses have had a  12 pay rise and i bow to nobody in my  admiration  for nurses uh in fact you know i learned  that  at the knee of my grandmother who was a  nurse and work nights at the  at the pilgrim hospital in boston the  the challenge is that the nation's  finances are  tight and whilst everybody else in the  public sector  is going to have a pay freeze we are  able to give a uh to propose a pay  rise for nurses at one percent  i entirely appreciate that that reflects  the difficult financial circumstances  that the  country is in but what i'd say to every  every nurse who's watching and everybody  who has worked so hard  is we have over this pandemic pulled  together  in an incredible way and i really hope  that we can continue doing that  both to deliver for patients and also to  support our nhs  and to welcome many many more nurses  into the nhs over the years ahead  thanks very much megan and thanks to all  that ends today's coronavirus briefing
 

